# Target C-II — the high-`t` train rectangle has macroscopic metric span, and the full rectangle admits a denser saturation lock

**Date:** 2026-09-17  
**Status:** proof-only high-`t` structural strengthening; no canonical promotion claimed  
**Scope:** canonical full-core/no-macroscopic-container Type-II branch with `t=(q-k)/D<=1.8687`  
**Dependencies:** canonical Theorem 10.6 exact train-track bounds; high-`t` complete rectangle; high-`t` phase collapse; phase-free path-region saturation; diameter-path Helly hub and one-sided switching; same-host diameter exclusion; full opposite-or-terminal nesting theorem; C67 diameter-degree bound  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_high_t_metric_grid_span_t18687.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The current saturation-grid theorem locks a linear family of actual diameter bars to a common inverse-`D` train grid at the level of exact selected-cut incidence. Its stated limitation is that selected-cut incidence is topological. This note adds a metric fact that is already implicit in canonical train geometry: distinct rhombi met along one train are separated by a fixed amount in the train's monotone coordinate.

Consequently the common grid is not an arbitrarily compressed pseudoline diagram. Its crossing centers have macroscopic physical span. On the already phase-collapsed synchronized grid, one common row-normal coordinate has span more than `0.06780D-o(D)` on every retained row, and one common column-normal coordinate has span more than `2.4248e-6 D-o(D)` across the synchronized row block on every retained column.

There is also a useful cardinality improvement that does not spend the phase-collapse factor. Applying the same hub-median synchronization directly to the earlier full complete rectangle `A_* x B_*` gives

\[
|E_*|,|A_1^*|>5.65\times10^{-6}D-o(D),
\qquad
|B_*|>0.3134D-o(D),
\]

with complete exact support incidence `E_* x A_1^*` and complete strict-rhombus incidence `A_1^* x B_*`. Every retained row has a common dominant-phase transverse span more than `0.27141D-o(D)` across `B_*`. Fixing a birth host gives a nested grid-spanning diameter rail of density `2.825e-6`, and fixing one physical destination leaves density `1.4125e-6`.

The phase-collapsed scaffold remains the one with inverse-`D` angle control. The full-rectangle scaffold is larger and metrically wider. Neither result alone proves C-II, but the synchronized architecture can no longer be treated as merely topological or potentially microscopically compressed.

---

## 1. Exact metric separation of crossing-rhombus centers on one train

Let `tau` be a train track in a finite strict unit-rhombus complex. Let `u` be a unit vector parallel to its track edges. Orient the train centerline so that

\[
h_u(x):=\det(u,x)
\]

strictly increases, as in canonical Theorem 10.6.

Each centerline segment crosses one unit rhombus from the midpoint of one track edge to the midpoint of the opposite track edge. If its displacement is `v`, then `|v|=1`, and Theorem 10.6 gives

\[
{\pi\over3}<\angle(u,v)<{2\pi\over3}.
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
\Delta h_u=\det(u,v)>{\sqrt3\over2}.
}
\tag{MG.1}
\]

Suppose `tau` crosses distinct train tracks `sigma_1,...,sigma_M`. Let

\[
z_1,\ldots,z_M
\]

be the centers of the corresponding crossing rhombi, listed in their order along the oriented centerline of `tau`.

### Lemma MG.1 — crossing-center separation

For every `1<=r<s<=M`,

\[
\boxed{
h_u(z_s)-h_u(z_r)>{\sqrt3\over2}(s-r).}
\tag{MG.2}
\]

In particular

\[
\boxed{
h_u(z_M)-h_u(z_1)>{\sqrt3\over2}(M-1).}
\tag{MG.3}
\]

### Proof

Index the rhombi traversed by `tau` in centerline order. The center of the `j`th rhombus lies halfway along the `j`th centerline segment. If two selected crossing rhombi are consecutive traversed rhombi, the center-to-center subpath consists of the second half of the first centerline segment and the first half of the next one. By (MG.1), its `h_u` increment is strictly greater than

\[
{1\over2}{\sqrt3\over2}+{1\over2}{\sqrt3\over2}
={\sqrt3\over2}.
\]

If unselected rhombi occur between them, every complete intervening centerline segment adds another amount greater than `sqrt(3)/2`. Thus consecutive selected crossing centers are separated in `h_u` by more than `sqrt(3)/2`. Summing gives (MG.2)--(MG.3). `square`

No phase-purity, diameter, KKT, or boundary hypothesis is used. This is a finite exact consequence of the strict rhombus angle bound.

---

## 2. Metric span of the full high-`t` complete rectangle

The high-`t` complete-rectangle theorem supplies train families

\[
A_*\times B_*
\]

with

\[
|A_*|>0.0000226D-o(D),
\qquad
|B_*|>0.3134D-o(D),
\tag{MG.4}
\]

such that every cross-pair meets in one strict rhombus and both shores are internally noncrossing. The rows `A_*` lie in one shrinking projective phase window.

Apply Lemma MG.1 to one row `a in A_*`. It crosses every column in `B_*`, so, with `u_a` its track-edge direction,

\[
\operatorname{span}_{h_{u_a}}\{z_{ab}:b\in B_*\}
>{\sqrt3\over2}(|B_*|-1).
\]

Hence uniformly in the row,

\[
\boxed{
\operatorname{span}_{h_{u_a}}\{z_{ab}:b\in B_*\}
>(0.2714123-o(1))D.
}
\tag{MG.5}
\]

Because all row projective phases lie in one `o(1)` window, choose consistently oriented unit representatives `u_a` and one limiting representative `u_*` with

\[
\max_{a\in A_*}\angle(u_a,u_*)=o(1).
\]

All crossing centers lie in the source hull, whose diameter is `D`. Therefore replacing `h_{u_a}` by the one common coordinate `h_{u_*}` changes any crossing-center difference by only `o(D)`. Thus

\[
\boxed{
\operatorname{span}_{h_{u_*}}\{z_{ab}:b\in B_*\}
>(0.2714123-o(1))D
\quad(a\in A_*).
}
\tag{MG.6}
\]

Every row of the full rectangle therefore traverses a common physical transverse width bounded below by a fixed fraction of the source diameter.

Similarly, for an individual column `b in B_*`, with its own track-edge direction `v_b`, Lemma MG.1 gives

\[
\boxed{
\operatorname{span}_{h_{v_b}}\{z_{ab}:a\in A_*\}
>(1.9572\times10^{-5}-o(1))D.
}
\tag{MG.7}
\]

The columns are not asserted here to lie in one shrinking phase window, so (MG.7) is an adapted-coordinate statement for each column separately.

---

## 3. The existing phase-collapsed synchronized scaffold has two common metric span directions

The phase-collapse theorem gives subfamilies

\[
A_0\subset A_* ,
\qquad B_0\subset B_*,
\]

with

\[
|A_0|>0.0000112D-o(D),
\qquad
|B_0|>0.0783D-o(D),
\tag{MG.8}
\]

and both projective phase spreads `O(1/D)`. Moreover every `A_0 x B_0` crossing has common order and

\[
{\pi\over3}<\theta_{ab}
<{\pi\over3}+{7.7\times10^6\over D}+o(D^{-1}).
\tag{MG.9}
\]

For each row, Lemma MG.1 gives

\[
\operatorname{span}_{h_{u_a}}\{z_{ab}:b\in B_0\}
>{\sqrt3\over2}(|B_0|-1),
\]

hence

\[
\boxed{
\operatorname{span}_{h_{u_a}}\{z_{ab}:b\in B_0\}
>(0.0678097-o(1))D.
}
\tag{MG.10}
\]

Now the row phase spread is `O(1/D)`, not merely `o(1)`. Choose one common oriented row direction `u_0`. Since any two crossing centers are at distance at most `D`, changing from `u_a` to `u_0` changes their `h`-difference by only `O(1)`. Therefore

\[
\boxed{
\operatorname{span}_{h_{u_0}}\{z_{ab}:b\in B_0\}
>(0.0678097-o(1))D
\quad(a\in A_0).
}
\tag{MG.11}
\]

The same argument applies to the column shore because its phase spread is also `O(1/D)`. On the whole `A_0` row set, every column has common-coordinate span

\[
\boxed{
>(9.6994\times10^{-6}-o(1))D.
}
\tag{MG.12}
\]

More importantly for the existing saturation-grid lock, its synchronized row subfamily `A_1 subset A_0` satisfies

\[
|A_1|>2.8\times10^{-6}D-o(D).
\]

Thus every `b in B_0` has, across the exact same `A_1` rows spanned by every synchronized diameter in `E_0`, common column-normal crossing-center span

\[
\boxed{
>(2.4248\times10^{-6}-o(1))D.
}
\tag{MG.13}
\]

Combining (MG.11), (MG.13), and the prior saturation-grid theorem, the same synchronized object now has all of the following simultaneously:

```text
|E_0| > 2.8e-6 D - o(D) actual diameter bars;
|A_1| > 2.8e-6 D - o(D) phase-collapsed rows;
|B_0| > 0.0783 D - o(D) phase-collapsed columns;
E_0 x A_1 complete in exact selected-cut support incidence;
A_1 x B_0 complete in strict-rhombus incidence and common crossing order;
all A_1 x B_0 angles = pi/3 + O(1/D);
every A_1 row has common row-normal B_0 crossing span > 0.0678097 D - o(D);
every B_0 column has common column-normal A_1 crossing span > 2.4248e-6 D - o(D).
```

So this scaffold is an inverse-`D` near-triangular grid with a macroscopic Euclidean footprint in two nonparallel common coordinates. The earlier qualification that `E_0 x A_1` itself is topological remains important: this theorem does **not** say that each diameter chord meets a row inside the `A_1 x B_0` grid patch.

---

## 4. Synchronizing region saturation before phase-collapse gives a larger exact incidence rectangle

The previous saturation-grid lock paid the phase-collapse thinning before choosing the hub side and median row block. That thinning is necessary for (MG.9), but not for exact support incidence.

Work instead with the full consecutive path block `A_*` from the complete-rectangle theorem. Remove the at most two selected cuts incident with the common diameter-routing hub region. One strict side of the hub contains

\[
>{|A_*|\over2}-O(1)
\]

rows. Reverse the path if necessary so this is a pre-hub side, and retain its hubward half. Call the resulting consecutive row block `A_1^*`. Then

\[
\boxed{
|A_1^*|>{0.0000226\over4}D-o(D)
=5.65\times10^{-6}D-o(D).
}
\tag{MG.14}
\]

If its first selected-path index is `j_0` and `L=|A_1^*|`, the same median-index argument as in the saturation-grid theorem gives at least `L` internal pre-hub regions `R_i` with `i<j_0`. Region saturation supplies an actual switching diameter `e_i` born in each such region. One-sided switching sends its other endpoint to the hubward side, so its exact selected-cut support contains every row in `A_1^*`.

Choosing `L` of these distinct bars gives a family `E_*` with

\[
\boxed{
|E_*|>5.65\times10^{-6}D-o(D)
}
\tag{MG.15}
\]

and

\[
\boxed{
E_*\times A_1^*
\text{ is complete in exact selected-cut support incidence.}
}
\tag{MG.16}
\]

Because `A_1^* subset A_*`, every row in `A_1^*` crosses every column in the unchanged full transverse shore `B_*`. Therefore

\[
\boxed{
A_1^*\times B_*
\text{ is complete in strict-rhombus incidence,}
}
\tag{MG.17}
\]

with

\[
\boxed{|B_*|>0.3134D-o(D).}
\tag{MG.18}
\]

The exact incidence product has coefficient

\[
(5.65\times10^{-6})(0.3134)
=1.77071\times10^{-6},
\]

so (MG.17) contains more than

\[
(1.77071\times10^{-6}-o(1))D^2
\]

distinct synchronized strict-rhombus crossings.

Every row in `A_1^*` retains the full metric span (MG.6):

\[
\boxed{
\operatorname{span}_{h_{u_*}}\{z_{ab}:b\in B_*\}
>(0.2714123-o(1))D.
}
\tag{MG.19}
\]

Every column `b in B_*`, in its adapted train coordinate, has across the consecutive `A_1^*` block

\[
\boxed{
\operatorname{span}_{h_{v_b}}\{z_{ab}:a\in A_1^*\}
>(4.8930\times10^{-6}-o(1))D.
}
\tag{MG.20}
\]

This is a larger unconditional support/grid synchronization than the phase-collapsed one. It deliberately does not claim inverse-`D` angle width on `A_1^* x B_*`.

---

## 5. Nested diameter rails inside the larger synchronized scaffold

The birth endpoints of `E_*` lie in distinct pre-hub path regions. Split them between the two physical birth host arcs. One class `E_*^{host}` has

\[
\boxed{
|E_*^{host}|>2.825\times10^{-6}D-o(D).
}
\tag{MG.21}
\]

Every one of these diameter bars still spans every row of `A_1^*`. Same-host diameter exclusion and the fixed-birth-host support-order theorem give a strictly nested support rail, with every partner on the opposite host or on `T_m`.

Fixing one of those two physical destinations leaves

\[
\boxed{
>1.4125\times10^{-6}D-o(D)
}
\tag{MG.22}
\]

strictly nested diameter bars. All of them continue to span the same `5.65e-6D` row block, whose every row crosses the same `0.3134D` transverse train family across the metric width (MG.19).

Using the canonical diameter-degree bound

\[
\Delta(H_D)\le(4\,3^{1/8}+o(1))D^{3/4},
\]

partner-collision thinning gives endpoint-disjoint, hence pairwise-crossing, synchronized diameter subfamilies with coefficients

\[
1.23125\times10^{-6},
\qquad
6.15627\times10^{-7},
\qquad
3.07813\times10^{-7}
\]

in front of `D^(1/4)` for `E_*`, the fixed-birth-host nested rail, and the fixed-destination nested rail respectively.

These endpoint-disjoint extractions are optional. The linear weak-thrackle rails in (MG.21)--(MG.22) retain much more cardinality and should be used whenever partner collisions are harmless.

---

## 6. What this proves and what it still does not prove

Two previous apparent losses are now separated cleanly.

1. **Metric compression is not available.** A complete strict-rhombus train grid with linear shores has linear physical crossing-center span because each train step advances by more than `sqrt(3)/2` in its exact monotone coordinate. In the phase-collapsed grid those train-adapted coordinates can be replaced by two common coordinates with only `O(1)` error.
2. **Phase-collapse is not needed for support synchronization.** Using the full rectangle first gives a `5.65e-6D x 0.3134D` exact support/grid lock and a `2.825e-6D` nested synchronized rail. Phase-collapse is needed only when the `pi/3+O(1/D)` angle information is used.

The remaining gap is narrower but real. Exact selected-cut incidence says a diameter chord crosses each row crosscut topologically. The theorem above does not force that straight chord to meet the row inside the macroscopic `B`-grid patch, and it does not produce a quantitative lower bound on the KKT multiplier of a synchronized diameter bar. A closing argument must add one of those missing bridges or derive a contradiction directly from the ordered boundary endpoints plus the now-metric train scaffold.

**Disposition:** converted the synchronized high-`t` train grid from a purely combinatorial rectangle into a quantitatively macroscopic metric scaffold, and doubled the synchronized row/diameter density while quadrupling the available transverse shore by delaying phase-collapse. C-II and Level I remain open.
